[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 1: Exemplary illustration of a user assessing the molecular function of a DNA sequence with a generic query. Left: The conversation between Genolator and the user. Right: A schematic overview of the backend process handling the user's query and parsing of the provided DNA sequence.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow schematic for a biological sequence/model interface system labeled “Genolator.” It is split by a vertical dashed divider into two main regions:\n\n- Left panel: “User Input via Interface”\n  - Shows a chat-style interaction between a user and Genolator.\n  - Visible user-provided DNA sequence: `ATGCGTACCTTACGCAGTACCGTTAGCATGCCGATTAACG`.\n  - The interface asks: “What types of molecular activities may be associated with the given protein?”\n  - A response bubble describes possible regulatory protein functions, including interaction with regulatory proteins, cellular signaling pathways, gene expression effects, and transcriptional regulation.\n  - Biological visuals include DNA double-helix graphics and a blue protein-like structure.\n  - User icon is shown as a scientist/doctor holding DNA.\n\n- Right panel: “Backend”\n  - Shows a model pipeline converting sequence input into embeddings and concatenated tokens.\n  - A translated amino-acid/protein sequence is shown: `MRTCERVPYSMPINT`.\n  - Components labeled:\n    - `Evo2`\n    - `ESMFold`\n    - `DNA Embedding`\n    - `AA Embedding`\n    - `Structure Embedding`\n    - `DNA Projector`\n    - `AA Projector`\n    - `Structure Projector`\n    - `Token Concatenation`\n  - Arrows indicate DNA input flows into Evo2 and sequence/structure processing; ESMFold produces a protein structure representation used for structure embedding.\n  - The final concatenated representation appears to feed back toward the interface response.\n\nBiological source objects shown include DNA sequences, amino-acid/protein sequence, DNA helices, and predicted protein structure imagery. The figure depicts transformations from DNA sequence to DNA embedding, amino-acid embedding, and protein structure embedding, followed by projection and token concatenation for a backend model that supports biological question answering."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 2: A. Illustration of a sample user interaction with Genolator, in which the user queries whether a sequence of interest  is  associated  with  a  specific  cellular  component,  and  Genolator  confirms  the  association.  B.  Schematic", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel schematic comparing model-question interactions for a protein/DNA-like sequence input.\n\nPanel A is labeled “Confirmation.” It shows a blue sequence bar at the top with the sequence `ATGCGTACCTTACGCAGTACCGTTAGCATGCCGATTAACG`. A user avatar asks whether the given protein could contain features allowing it to associate with lipid-rich cellular boundaries. A Genolator/model response states that hydrophobic stretches and predicted transmembrane domains in the amino acid sequence suggest the protein may associate with lipid bilayers. Biological visuals include a DNA helix held by the user and a protein/DNA-style molecular illustration near the model response.\n\nPanel B is labeled “Denial.” It shows the same blue sequence bar. A user avatar asks whether observed features suggest a role in metabolic enzymatic catalysis, such as carbohydrate breakdown. The Genolator/model response says no data indicate the protein contains features associated with metabolic enzymatic catalysis such as carbohydrate breakdown. Biological visuals again include a DNA helix and a protein/DNA-style molecular illustration.\n\nA dashed vertical line separates the two panels. The figure illustrates contrasting confirmation and denial outputs from a biological sequence interpretation interface/model, using the same sequence input but different biological-function queries."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 3: Bar plot displaying the Matthews Correlation CoePicient (MCC) achieved by each model across diPerent Gene Ontology (GO) Aspects. The x-axis denotes the various models evaluated, while the y-axis indicates the MCC values. Each colored bar represents a diPerent GO Aspect: Blue - Biological Process, Purple - Molecular Function, Yellow Cellular Component", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel grouped bar chart titled “Matthews Correlation Coefficient across all Models and GO Aspects.”\n\nVisible labels:\n- X-axis: “Model”\n- Y-axis: “MCC”\n- Legend: Biological Process, Molecular Function, Cellular Component\n- Models shown: Genolator (full), Genolator (TP), GPT 4.1, XGBoost\n\nContent:\n- Bars compare Matthews Correlation Coefficient values across three Gene Ontology aspects for each model.\n- Biological Process is shown in blue, Molecular Function in purple, Cellular Component in gold.\n- Approximate values:\n  - Genolator (full): high MCC across all aspects, around 0.94-0.98.\n  - Genolator (TP): lower than full Genolator, around 0.84-0.90.\n  - GPT 4.1: much lower MCC, around 0.13-0.27.\n  - XGBoost: high MCC, around 0.92-0.95.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No biological objects are visually depicted.\n- The biological categories are Gene Ontology aspects: Biological Process, Molecular Function, and Cellular Component.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure visualizes model performance metrics as grouped bars.\n- No model architecture, workflow diagram, microscopy image, molecular structure, or interface is shown.\n\nMain finding:\n- Genolator (full) and XGBoost show the strongest MCC performance across Gene Ontology aspects.\n- GPT 4.1 performs substantially worse than the other models in this comparison."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 13, "caption": "Figure 3: t-SNE visualization for a selection of Biological Process Gene Ontology (GO) terms 29 , generated using the last hidden  states  from  the  fine-tuned  Llama 33 model.  Each  subplot  represents  a  diPerent  GO  term 29 ,  displaying embeddings projected into two dimensions (t-SNE 1 and t-SNE 2 axes). Data points are colored as follows: yellow", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA multi-panel t-SNE visualization titled “t-SNE visualization for Biological Process (Confirmation/Denial).” The figure contains 12 scatterplot panels arranged in a 4 x 3 grid. Each panel shows the same t-SNE embedding with axes labeled `t-SNE 1` and `t-SNE 2`, overlaid with category labels for one GO biological process term.\n\nVisible panels / GO terms:\n- regulation of DNA-templated transcription\n- chromatin organization\n- cytoplasmic translation\n- programmed cell death\n- mitotic cell cycle\n- DNA repair\n- reproductive process\n- cell differentiation\n- anatomical structure development\n- signaling\n- immune system process\n- inflammatory response\n\nLegend categories:\n- Confirmed: yellow points\n- Denied: magenta/pink points\n- Unclassified: blue points\n\nBiological source objects: gene ontology biological process annotations or terms mapped onto embedded biological entities, likely genes or proteins, though the specific source objects are not explicitly stated in the image.\n\nTransformation/model interface: two-dimensional t-SNE dimensionality reduction embedding. Each GO term panel highlights confirmed and denied examples against a background of unclassified points.\n\nVisible finding: Most points are unclassified blue across all panels. Confirmed yellow points cluster in term-specific regions, especially for signaling, immune system process, programmed cell death, cell differentiation, and mitotic cell cycle. Denied magenta points appear mainly in separate clusters on the right and upper-right portions of the embedding, varying by GO term."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Figure 4: t-SNE visualization for a selection of Molecular Function Gene Ontology (GO) terms 29 , generated using the last hidden  states  from  the  fine-tuned  Llama 33 model.  Each  subplot  represents  a  diPerent  GO  term 29 ,  displaying embeddings projected into two dimensions (t-SNE 1 and t-SNE 2 axes). Data points are colored as follows: yellow ('Confirmed') indicates the model associates the corresponding gene with the GO term 29 ; purple ('Denied') indicates the model denies the association; blue ('Unclassified') indicates genes for which no association was queried.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific visualization showing t-SNE embeddings for molecular function annotations, titled “t-SNE visualization for Molecular Function (Confirmation/Denial).”\n\nIt contains 12 scatterplot panels arranged in a 4 x 3 grid. Each panel shows the same t-SNE coordinate space with axes labeled `t-SNE 1` and `t-SNE 2`, and points colored by label status. The legend indicates three categories: `Confirmed` in yellow/orange, `Denied` in magenta/purple, and `Unclassified` in blue.\n\nVisible panel titles are Gene Ontology molecular-function terms:\n\n- `GO term: DNA binding`\n- `GO term: RNA binding`\n- `GO term: histone binding`\n- `GO term: transcription regulator activity`\n- `GO term: protein folding chaperone`\n- `GO term: structural molecule activity`\n- `GO term: transporter activity`\n- `GO term: receptor ligand activity`\n- `GO term: molecular transducer activity`\n- `GO term: ligase activity`\n- `GO term: hydrolase activity`\n- `GO term: transferase activity`\n\nThe biological source objects appear to be molecular function annotations or gene/protein-associated GO terms represented as embedded data points. The transformation shown is dimensionality reduction by t-SNE into two dimensions. The model/interface context is a confirmation/denial classification or labeling interface over GO molecular-function terms, separating confirmed, denied, and unclassified examples in the embedding space.\n\nVisible findings: most points are unclassified blue across all panels, while confirmed yellow/orange and denied magenta/purple points form localized clusters or regions depending on the GO term. Some terms such as transporter activity, hydrolase activity, and transferase activity show more visible denied magenta clusters than terms like histone binding or transcription regulator activity."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 15, "caption": "Figure 5: t-SNE visualization for a selection of Cellular Component Gene Ontology (GO) terms 29 , generated using the last  hidden  states  from  the  fine-tuned  Llama 33 model.  Each  subplot  represents  a  diPerent  GO  term 29 ,  displaying embeddings projected into two dimensions (t-SNE 1 and t-SNE 2 axes). Data points are colored as follows: yellow ('Confirmed') indicates the model associates the corresponding gene with the GO term 29 ; purple ('Denied') indicates the model denies the association; blue ('Unclassified') indicates genes for which no association was queried.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific visualization titled “t-SNE visualization for Cellular Component (Confirmation/Denial).” It shows 12 t-SNE scatter plots arranged in a 4 x 3 grid.\n\nVisible panels are labeled by Gene Ontology cellular component terms:\n\n- GO term: chromosome\n- GO term: nuclear chromosome\n- GO term: nucleolus\n- GO term: nucleus\n- GO term: nucleoplasm\n- GO term: cytoplasmic vesicle\n- GO term: cytoskeleton\n- GO term: cytosol\n- GO term: plasma membrane\n- GO term: extracellular matrix\n- GO term: extracellular region\n- GO term: extracellular space\n\nEach panel plots points in a 2D t-SNE embedding with axes labeled “t-SNE 1” and “t-SNE 2.” A legend indicates class labels:\n\n- Confirmed: yellow/orange\n- Denied: magenta/purple\n- Unclassified: blue\n\nThe biological source objects appear to be items associated with Gene Ontology cellular component annotations, likely proteins or genes, embedded into a shared feature space and visualized by t-SNE. The transformation shown is dimensionality reduction into two t-SNE coordinates.\n\nFindings visible from the figure: unclassified blue points dominate all panels, while confirmed and denied examples appear as colored subsets localized to different regions of the embedding depending on the GO term. Nuclear/chromosomal terms show confirmed points concentrated mostly in upper-left/central clusters; plasma membrane, cytoskeleton, and extracellular-related terms show denied points more prominently in right-side clusters."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 6: t-SNE 43 visualizations of the confirmation questions, generated from the last hidden states of the fine-tuned Llama model. Each subplot corresponds to one of the three GO aspects 29 . Yellow points represent datapoints related to relevant (confirmed) questions for the respective GO aspect 29 , while blue points denote unrelated datapoints.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a three-panel t-SNE visualization for Gene Ontology (GO) aspects, comparing two classes labeled “related” and “unrelated.”\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left: “GO Aspect: biological_process”\n- Middle: “GO Aspect: molecular_function”\n- Right: “GO Aspect: cellular_component”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Overall title: “t-SNE visualization for GO Aspects (related/unrelated)”\n- Each panel has x-axis “t-SNE 1” and y-axis “t-SNE 2”\n- Legend in each panel:\n  - Yellow/orange points: “related”\n  - Blue points: “unrelated”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Gene Ontology aspect categories are shown: biological process, molecular function, and cellular component.\n- The plotted objects appear to be embedded GO-related pairs or examples labeled as related vs unrelated, but the exact biological entities are not specified in the image.\n\nTransformations/model interface:\n- High-dimensional representations were transformed into 2D using t-SNE.\n- No specific embedding model, neural network, or interface is named in the figure.\n\nVisible findings:\n- The related and unrelated classes overlap substantially in all three GO aspects.\n- Some regional clustering is visible, especially yellow/orange concentrations in parts of each panel, but there is no clean separation between the two labels.\n- The cellular_component panel shows a large yellow/orange band toward the lower-left/lower-middle area with blue points more dispersed across the upper and right regions."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 19, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific data visualization with panels labeled A, B, C, and D.\n\nPanel A: Bar chart titled “Evaluation of Generic QA Samples on Slim Go Terms (Generated Answers vs. Ground Truth).” It compares fractions of GO term matching categories across three GO aspects: Biological Process, Molecular Function, and Cellular Component. Categories shown are “Overlapping GO Terms,” “Exact Matching GO Terms,” and “No Matching GO Terms.” The main finding visible is that overlapping GO terms dominate for all three aspects, exact matches are very low, and no-match fractions are highest for Biological Process.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart comparing “Ground Truth Testset” versus “Generated” counts per GO term for the Cellular Component aspect. X-axis contains GO cellular component terms; y-axis is count. Paired blue and magenta bars show term-wise differences between ground truth and generated outputs.\n\nPanel C: Wide bar chart comparing ground truth and generated counts per GO term for Biological Process. Numerous GO biological process terms are shown on the x-axis with rotated labels. The chart shows substantial term-specific variation, with some generated counts much higher than ground truth for certain processes.\n\nPanel D: Bar chart comparing ground truth and generated counts per GO term for Molecular Function. GO molecular function terms are shown on the x-axis with rotated labels, including binding and activity-related terms. Paired bars show generated versus ground-truth frequency differences.\n\nBiological source objects: Gene Ontology terms grouped by GO aspect: Biological Process, Molecular Function, and Cellular Component.\n\nTransformations / analysis: Generated answer GO terms are compared against a ground-truth test set using overlap, exact-match, and no-match fractions, plus per-term count distributions.\n\nModel/interface context visible: The figure evaluates generated answers against ground truth, but no specific model architecture, dataset name beyond “Ground Truth Testset,” or user interface is shown.\n\nFindings: Generated outputs broadly overlap with ground-truth GO terms, but exact GO-term matches are rare. Per-term distributions differ between generated and ground-truth counts, with several GO terms over- or under-represented in generated outputs."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 20, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel scientific bar-chart figure comparing median attention values across GO-term categories.\n\nPanel A:\n- Label: “A”\n- Title: “KMeans Split: Median Attentions Across All GO Terms”\n- X-axis: “Attention Type”\n- Y-axis: “Median Attention”\n- Attention types shown: Evo2 Attention, ESM Attention, GAE Attention\n- Legend/Fraction Group:\n  - Exact Matching GO Terms\n  - No Matching GO Terms\n  - Overlapping GO Terms\n- Findings visible: Evo2 attention has the highest median values, around 0.095-0.10 across all GO-term groups. ESM attention is much lower, around 0.014-0.015. GAE attention is lowest, around 0.004-0.005.\n\nPanel B:\n- Label: “B”\n- Title: “Gene Split: Median Attentions Across All GO Terms”\n- X-axis: “Attention Type”\n- Y-axis: “Median Attention”\n- Same three attention types and GO-term fraction groups as Panel A.\n- Findings visible: Evo2 attention again has the highest median values, around 0.061-0.064. ESM attention varies more by group, with exact matching GO terms higher than the other groups. GAE attention remains lowest, around 0.006-0.007.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- GO terms are the biological annotation objects referenced.\n- The plotted groups distinguish exact matching, no matching, and overlapping GO terms.\n\nModel interfaces/representations:\n- The figure compares attention-derived quantities from Evo2, ESM, and GAE models or methods.\n\nTransformations:\n- Median attention values are computed across all GO terms and grouped by split strategy: KMeans split in Panel A and gene split in Panel B."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 31, "caption": "Figure 9: QA Dataset creation workflow", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a vertical workflow/flowchart for generating high-quality biological question-answer datasets.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Top node: “MANE Select Release 1.4”\n- Three input/retrieval steps:\n  - “Fetch Protein Structures from AlphaFold”\n  - “Retrieve Amino Acid Sequence”\n  - “Retrieve DNA Sequence”\n- Annotation step:\n  - “Annotate Genes with Gene Ontologies for: Biological Process, Molecular Function, Cellular Component”\n- Prompt construction:\n  - “Engineering of System Prompts for each GO Aspect and Task: Confirmation, Denial, Generic”\n- Few-shot setup:\n  - “Handcraft Few-shot Examples from 2-3 unrelated genes per system prompt”\n- Generation:\n  - “Generation of 56 Test QA Pairs for each Gene and GO Aspect using GPT-4.1”\n- Model/tool interface:\n  - “LLMOps: Tracing and Analytics in Azure Phoenix hosted on Azure”\n- Human review:\n  - “Blind Review by Two Researchers via Annotation in Arize Phoenix hosted on Azure”\n- Decision diamond:\n  - “Quality accepted”\n  - “No” loops back to refactoring of system prompts and few-shots\n  - “Yes” continues to QA generation\n- Output generation branches:\n  - “Generic: 1 QA pair per gene and aspect”\n  - “Denial: 3 QA pairs per gene and aspect”\n  - “Confirmation: 3 QA pairs per gene and aspect”\n- Combined dataset:\